From 08cb92f3ce0b23aca065e4ea8ab8f0782265127e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PePe Amengual <2208324+jamengual@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 11:02:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] rmlock: wake all waiters when the last tracked reader leaves

A writer waiting for active readers sleeps on the rmlock's turnstile and
the last reader wakes it with turnstile_signal(), assuming the writer is
the only waiter. It is not: epoch_block_handler_preempt() puts a thread
waiting for an epoch to drain on the turnstile of the lock that a thread
in the epoch section is blocked on. When that thread is an rmlock writer
waiting for readers, the epoch waiter joins the rmlock's turnstile, and
only one of the waiters is woken. The other sleeps forever on an
ownerless turnstile.

Seen on Kubernetes nodes, where pod churn creates and destroys epairs
while pf filters traffic: pfi_attach_ifnet_event() and
pfi_detach_ifnet_event() take pf's rules write lock inside the net
epoch, and if_alloc_domain() and if_detach_internal() wait for the
epoch while holding ifnet_sx and ifnet_detach_sx. After a burst of pod
starts and stops two ifconfig processes slept on the turnstile of
"pf rulesets" with no owner, no readers and no writer left; jail
removal then blocked on ifnet_detach_sx while holding allprison, and
every fork and exit queued behind allproc.

Use turnstile_broadcast(). The writer loops while readers remain, and
epoch waiters return to ck_epoch's scan, so spurious wakeups are
harmless.
---
 sys/kern/kern_rmlock.c | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sys/kern/kern_rmlock.c b/sys/kern/kern_rmlock.c
index 7206572ff..df64586e0 100644
--- a/sys/kern/kern_rmlock.c
+++ b/sys/kern/kern_rmlock.c
@@ -494,7 +494,19 @@ _rm_unlock_hard(struct thread *td,struct rm_priotracker *tracker)
 
 		ts = turnstile_lookup(&rm->lock_object);
 
-		turnstile_signal(ts, TS_EXCLUSIVE_QUEUE);
+		/*
+		 * Wake every waiter, not just one. The writer waiting for
+		 * this reader is not the only thread that can be on the
+		 * turnstile: epoch_block_handler_preempt() puts threads
+		 * waiting for an epoch to drain on the turnstile of the lock
+		 * an epoch reader is blocked on, here the writer's (e.g.
+		 * pf's rules lock, write-locked inside the net epoch). With
+		 * turnstile_signal() only one of them woke; the others slept
+		 * on an ownerless turnstile forever, holding whatever they
+		 * held (ifnet_sx, ifnet_detach_sx). Every waiter rechecks its
+		 * condition: the writer loops while readers remain.
+		 */
+		turnstile_broadcast(ts, TS_EXCLUSIVE_QUEUE);
 		turnstile_unpend(ts);
 		turnstile_chain_unlock(&rm->lock_object);
 	} else
